{"name":"Spinnaker Blue","hex":"#018cb6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spinnaker","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sail supplemental to the mainsail, especially a triangular one, used on yachts for running before the wind."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To sail using a spinnaker"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spinnaker"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spinnaker","blocks":[{"text":"A spinnaker is a sail designed specifically for sailing off the wind on courses between a reach to downwind. Spinnakers are constructed of lightweight fabric, usually nylon, and are often brightly colored. They may be designed to perform best as either a reaching or a running spinnaker, by the shaping of the panels and seams. They are attached at only three points and said to be flown."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Amante_Choate_48_photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg/330px-Amante_Choate_48_photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spinnaker"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Bear_of_Britain_spinnaker.jpg/500px-Bear_of_Britain_spinnaker.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Bear_of_Britain_spinnaker.jpg/1280px-Bear_of_Britain_spinnaker.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Bear_of_Britain_spinnaker.jpg/500px-Bear_of_Britain_spinnaker.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bear of Britain, a Farr 52 with masthead spinnaker in front of Calshot Spit","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABear_of_Britain_spinnaker.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/420_Class_Dinghies_with_spinnakers.jpg/500px-420_Class_Dinghies_with_spinnakers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/420_Class_Dinghies_with_spinnakers.jpg/1280px-420_Class_Dinghies_with_spinnakers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/420_Class_Dinghies_with_spinnakers.jpg/500px-420_Class_Dinghies_with_spinnakers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"420 class dinghies with symmetric spinnakers.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A4